    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.misc\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.11.x\n\n### Description\n\nI'm not sure whether this should be considered a bug or an intentional feature. The issue is that when I zoomed into a buffer, the background color changed due to the difference between the `Normal` and `FloatNormal` highlights. Simply changing `FloatNormal` to match `Normal` would disrupt the styles of many floating windows. Since `MiniMisc.zoom` is mainly used in normal buffers (as documented), should we set `'winhighlight'` to `\"FloatNormal:Normal\"` after zooming in, or more generally, provide a way to run callbacks after zooming in?\n\n### Reproduction\n\nYou can also find the files [here](https://github.com/loichyan/mini.nvim/commit/6d95d7c0f8d245a14b19220b6160a3f828edc07c).\n\n```lua\n-- Clone latest 'mini.nvim' (requires Git CLI installed)\nvim.cmd('echo \"Installing `mini.nvim`\" | redraw')\nlocal mini_path = vim.fn.stdpath('data') .. '/site/pack/deps/start/mini.nvim'\nlocal clone_cmd = { 'git', 'clone', '--depth=1', 'https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim', mini_path }\nvim.fn.system(clone_cmd)\nvim.cmd('echo \"`mini.nvim` is installed\" | redraw')\n\n-- Make sure 'mini.nvim' is available\nvim.cmd('packadd mini.nvim')\nrequire('mini.deps').setup()\n\n-- Add extra setup steps needed to reproduce the behavior\n-- Use `MiniDeps.add('user/repo')` to install another plugin from GitHub\n\nrequire('mini.misc').setup()\n-- Set a high contrast highlight for floatings.\n-- Though normally only background color is slightly different.\nvim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'NormalFloat', { fg = 'red', bg = 'green' })\n```",
